# Findings gitleaks is right to raise and wrong about, pinned one at a time.
#
# A fingerprint is commit:file:rule:line, so an entry here excuses exactly one
# line of one commit. Anything new, anywhere else, still fails the build: this
# is not the rule being switched off.
#
# The scrub job checks out with fetch-depth 0, which fetches every branch, and
# gitleaks reads all of it. A finding on any branch therefore fails the build on
# every other branch, which is why these turn up on work that never went near
# the file.

# core/src/passwords.rs held a test that proves the secret scrubber finds
# secrets, and everything in the list it works through is invented: "hunter22",
# "geheim123", "sk-abc123def". The last of those looks enough like an API key
# for the generic-api-key rule to match it, on two adjacent lines. Nothing there
# was ever real, the file does not exist on main, and a later commit on that
# branch replaced the fixture anyway.
